WEBVTT

00:00:00.064 --> 00:00:04.307
Large experimentation platforms run A/B tests all the time.

00:00:04.626 --> 00:00:09.436
Yet what teams learn from them is often scattered across reports and dashboards.

00:00:09.768 --> 00:00:14.784
Can we turn those results into an institutional memory that an agent can rely on?

00:00:15.395 --> 00:00:17.859
That question motivates ExperimentWiki.

00:00:18.191 --> 00:00:22.727
We also want to know whether the system can update its conclusions as new evidence

00:00:22.727 --> 00:00:27.150
arrives, and whether it can spot and repair mistakes in its own workflow.

00:00:28.228 --> 00:00:31.143
This diagram shows how the pieces fit together.

00:00:31.488 --> 00:00:33.901
Start on the left, with Experiment Memory.

00:00:34.246 --> 00:00:38.179
Within each experiment, we compare the treatment groups with the control group.

00:00:38.512 --> 00:00:41.530
We record what changed, which outcomes moved,

00:00:41.811 --> 00:00:43.824
the size and direction of the effects,

00:00:44.014 --> 00:00:45.628
and the uncertainty around them.

00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:48.308
Now follow the arrows into the wiki.

00:00:48.628 --> 00:00:51.401
Deterministic programs compute the statistics,

00:00:51.553 --> 00:00:54.713
store the results, and use templates to write pages.

00:00:55.058 --> 00:00:59.685
The language model reads those pages when it reasons about a question or advises a

00:00:59.685 --> 00:01:00.138
team.

00:01:01.241 --> 00:01:02.275
Here is a simpler view.

00:01:02.607 --> 00:01:06.282
Suppose a setting was changed in several experiments.

00:01:06.628 --> 00:01:07.764
Did clicks tend to rise?

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:12.671
The experiments may have served different populations or received different

00:01:12.671 --> 00:01:13.692
amounts of traffic.

00:01:14.037 --> 00:01:19.027
We examine how strong and consistent the evidence is across those settings.

00:01:19.632 --> 00:01:23.604
A page for a parameter shows which outcomes moved when it changed.

00:01:23.923 --> 00:01:27.251
A page for an outcome lets us ask the reverse question:

00:01:27.647 --> 00:01:29.789
which changes have affected this metric?

00:01:30.096 --> 00:01:32.933
A recurring pattern may suggest a mechanism.

00:01:33.279 --> 00:01:35.820
That explanation is still a hypothesis.

00:01:36.436 --> 00:01:39.145
The researcher agent can also identify a gap.

00:01:39.478 --> 00:01:42.380
Suppose two settings have always changed together.

00:01:42.738 --> 00:01:44.880
Their separate effects are unknown.

00:01:45.212 --> 00:01:50.125
The agent can propose a factorial experiment that varies them independently.

00:01:50.457 --> 00:01:53.836
It can also register a prediction before the next result arrives,

00:01:54.143 --> 00:01:57.096
so we can check it against evidence it has never seen.

00:01:58.186 --> 00:01:59.388
How does the system learn?

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:03.885
On the left, new data arrives while the programs stay fixed.

00:02:04.192 --> 00:02:07.029
We grade findings using earlier experiments,

00:02:07.362 --> 00:02:09.916
then check their predictions against later results.

00:02:10.235 --> 00:02:14.246
We can also set historical experiments aside for an independent check.

00:02:14.853 --> 00:02:16.711
The chart shows why the grades matter.

00:02:17.082 --> 00:02:21.168
Findings with stronger evidence were more likely to hold in the predicted

00:02:21.168 --> 00:02:21.763
direction.

00:02:22.095 --> 00:02:26.258
On the right, the data can stay fixed while we investigate how an answer was

00:02:26.258 --> 00:02:26.969
produced.

00:02:28.049 --> 00:02:31.956
Consider a question that needs evidence from across experiments.

00:02:32.301 --> 00:02:34.701
If the agent looks up just one experiment,

00:02:35.033 --> 00:02:38.760
its answer can miss the point even when the arithmetic is correct.

00:02:39.395 --> 00:02:41.962
We record the path from the question to the answer.

00:02:42.294 --> 00:02:45.673
An independent program recomputes the statistics.

00:02:45.993 --> 00:02:49.616
A language model checks the trace for mistakes in tool use.

00:02:49.975 --> 00:02:53.560
Human users judge whether the answer addresses their question.

00:02:54.228 --> 00:02:58.816
When a check flags a routing error, the system can propose a fix and test it on

00:02:58.816 --> 00:02:59.952
held-out cases.

00:03:00.298 --> 00:03:03.445
It keeps the change only if those checks still pass.

00:03:03.777 --> 00:03:06.512
New evidence revises what the wiki says.

00:03:06.857 --> 00:03:10.339
These audits repair how the system reaches an answer.

00:03:11.395 --> 00:03:12.209
Why build a wiki?

00:03:12.555 --> 00:03:15.457
With a typical retrieval-augmented generation system,

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:20.727
the agent retrieves passages and interprets them when a question comes in.

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:25.083
A related question can require the same analysis all over again.

00:03:25.674 --> 00:03:30.506
ExperimentWiki does the cross-experiment comparison ahead of time and keeps the

00:03:30.506 --> 00:03:32.158
synthesis on a page.

00:03:32.478 --> 00:03:34.607
An agent can consult that page again,

00:03:34.952 --> 00:03:39.604
see where the evidence conflicts, and revisit the conclusion when a new experiment

00:03:39.604 --> 00:03:40.212
comes in.
